# Context

## Key Trade-offs
- Newest-first list order was a judgment call: the module draft
  served insertion order, but the contract documents newest first
  twice (operation description and items schema). The backend is
  the conformance target, so it follows the documented semantics
  the participant reads.
- The isolation advisory (OAL-PACK-ISOLATION-ADVISORY) is
  expected, not suppressed: this build runs scenario behavior
  trusted-local and the validator says so.
- Cursor is the last served clip id; the contract calls it
  opaque, and ids are stable, so this satisfies opacity while
  staying deterministic.

## Gotchas
- The backend imports BehaviorHttpError by relative path into
  packages/; a pack resolves no bare workspace specifiers. ESLint
  ignores packs/** for this reason (no tsconfig project covers
  them); testkit imports keep them type-checked.
- idsAdapter pads to 8 digits (clip_00000001); the state schema
  pattern ^clip_[0-9a-z]+$ admits it.

